(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("k8s-ops",{"k8s-ops/the-control-plane-in-depth":{"html":"\u003cp>Using Kubernetes means writing manifests. Running it means keeping the control plane healthy. When you operate clusters you need to know exactly what the API server, etcd, scheduler and controller manager do, how they depend on each other, and what breaks when each one fails. This knowledge is what lets you stay calm when \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> stops answering.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Trace a request through the API server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain what etcd stores and why it matters most\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describe the scheduler and controller manager roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Predict the effect of each component failing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-api-server\">The API server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every read and write goes through the \u003cstrong>API server\u003c/strong>. It authenticates the caller, authorises the request (RBAC), runs admission controllers that can mutate or reject objects, validates the object, and stores it in etcd. Nothing else writes to etcd. Kubelets, controllers and users are all clients of this one gateway. It is stateless and can run as several replicas behind a load balancer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"etcd\">etcd\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>etcd\u003c/strong> is a consistent key-value store holding all cluster state: every object you create. It uses a consensus protocol and needs a majority of members to work, so three or five members are typical. It is sensitive to slow disks and network latency: slow writes show up as slow API calls. If etcd is lost with no backup, the cluster state is lost, although running containers continue for a while.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scheduler-and-controllers\">Scheduler and controllers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>scheduler\u003c/strong> watches for pods with no node and picks one by filtering (resources, taints, affinity) and scoring. The \u003cstrong>controller manager\u003c/strong> runs control loops: the Deployment controller creates ReplicaSets, the ReplicaSet controller creates pods, the node controller marks unreachable nodes, and many more. Each loop compares desired with actual state and acts to close the gap. The cloud controller manager does the same for cloud load balancers and routes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"on-each-node\">On each node\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>kubelet\u003c/strong> receives pod specs for its node, asks the container runtime to run them, runs probes and reports status. \u003cstrong>kube-proxy\u003c/strong> (or an alternative network layer) implements service routing. A node can keep running existing pods if the control plane is down, but nothing new is scheduled and nothing is repaired.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-breaks-when\">What breaks when\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>API server down: no changes and no \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code>; existing pods keep running. etcd down or slow: the API server fails or lags. Scheduler down: new pods stay Pending. Controller manager down: no scaling, no replacement of failed pods. Kubelet down on a node: that node goes NotReady and its pods are rescheduled after a timeout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> The scheduler is down for ten minutes. A Deployment is scaled from 3 to 6. What do you see?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Three new pods are created but stay Pending without a node, until the scheduler returns.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Controllers create, the scheduler places.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-kubectl-hangs\">Incident: kubectl hangs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Engineers could not run any kubectl command and the dashboard timed out.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes\ncurl -sk https://10.0.0.10:6443/livez?verbose | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Unable to connect to the server: net/http: request canceled (Client.Timeout exceeded)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sk https://10.0.0.10:6443/livez?verbose | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[-]etcd failed: reason withheld\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">livez check failed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The health endpoint pointed at etcd. Disk latency on the etcd members had risen after a noisy neighbour job. Moving etcd to dedicated fast disks and alerting on write latency restored the API; running workloads had stayed up the whole time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating the control plane as an afterthought\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running etcd on slow shared disks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Single API server in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No monitoring of control plane latency\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-api-server","text":"The API server"},{"id":"etcd","text":"etcd"},{"id":"scheduler-and-controllers","text":"Scheduler and controllers"},{"id":"on-each-node","text":"On each node"},{"id":"what-breaks-when","text":"What breaks when"},{"id":"incident-kubectl-hangs","text":"Incident: kubectl hangs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes components","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/components/"},{"t":"Administer a cluster","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/"}],"text":"Using Kubernetes means writing manifests. Running it means keeping the control plane healthy. When you operate clusters you need to know exactly what the API server, etcd, scheduler and controller manager do, how they depend on each other, and what breaks when each one fails. This knowledge is what lets you stay calm when kubectl stops answering. What you will learn Trace a request through the API server Explain what etcd stores and why it matters most Describe the scheduler and controller manager roles Predict the effect of each component failing The API server Every read and write goes through the API server . It authenticates the caller, authorises the request (RBAC), runs admission controllers that can mutate or reject objects, validates the object, and stores it in etcd. Nothing else writes to etcd. Kubelets, controllers and users are all clients of this one gateway. It is stateless and can run as several replicas behind a load balancer. etcd etcd is a consistent key-value store holding all cluster state: every object you create. It uses a consensus protocol and needs a majority of members to work, so three or five members are typical. It is sensitive to slow disks and network latency: slow writes show up as slow API calls. If etcd is lost with no backup, the cluster state is lost, although running containers continue for a while. Scheduler and controllers The scheduler watches for pods with no node and picks one by filtering (resources, taints, affinity) and scoring. The controller manager runs control loops: the Deployment controller creates ReplicaSets, the ReplicaSet controller creates pods, the node controller marks unreachable nodes, and many more. Each loop compares desired with actual state and acts to close the gap. The cloud controller manager does the same for cloud load balancers and routes. On each node The kubelet receives pod specs for its node, asks the container runtime to run them, runs probes and reports status. kube-proxy (or an alternative network layer) implements service routing. A node can keep running existing pods if the control plane is down, but nothing new is scheduled and nothing is repaired. What breaks when API server down: no changes and no kubectl ; existing pods keep running. etcd down or slow: the API server fails or lags. Scheduler down: new pods stay Pending. Controller manager down: no scaling, no replacement of failed pods. Kubelet down on a node: that node goes NotReady and its pods are rescheduled after a timeout. Predict first. The scheduler is down for ten minutes. A Deployment is scaled from 3 to 6. What do you see? Your guess Answer: Three new pods are created but stay Pending without a node, until the scheduler returns. Controllers create, the scheduler places. Incident: kubectl hangs Incident Engineers could not run any kubectl command and the dashboard timed out. $ kubectl get nodes Unable to connect to the server: net/http: request canceled (Client.Timeout exceeded) $ curl -sk https://10.0.0.10:6443/livez?verbose | tail -3 [-]etcd failed: reason withheld livez check failed The health endpoint pointed at etcd. Disk latency on the etcd members had risen after a noisy neighbour job. Moving etcd to dedicated fast disks and alerting on write latency restored the API; running workloads had stayed up the whole time. Common mistakes Treating the control plane as an afterthought Running etcd on slow shared disks Single API server in production No monitoring of control plane latency"},"k8s-ops/etcd-backup-and-restore":{"html":"\u003cp>If you lose etcd and have no backup, you lose the definition of your cluster. A backup you have never restored is only a hope. This lesson covers taking snapshots, storing them safely, restoring into a new cluster, and testing the whole procedure on a schedule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Take an etcd snapshot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Store and protect backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restore from a snapshot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test restores and know what a backup does not cover\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"taking-a-snapshot\">Taking a snapshot\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>etcdctl\u003c/code> with the endpoints and client certificates of your cluster:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-$(date +%F).db \\\n  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\\n  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\\n  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\\n  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\netcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2024-05-14.db -w table\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span> +%F).db \\\n  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\\n  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\\n  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\\n  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\netcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2024-05-14.db -w table\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The status command shows the revision, key count and size, which proves the file is readable. Managed Kubernetes services hide etcd; use the provider&#39;s backup features or a tool that backs up objects and volumes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"store-it-well\">Store it well\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Copy snapshots off the node at once, to storage in another location, encrypted and with limited access, because etcd holds Secrets. Keep several generations and a retention policy. Schedule snapshots often enough for your recovery point: hourly is common. Alert if the newest snapshot is older than expected.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restoring\">Restoring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Restoring creates a new data directory from the snapshot with \u003ccode>etcdutl snapshot restore\u003c/code>, then the etcd static pod or service is pointed at it. On a multi-member cluster, restore every member from the same snapshot with the new cluster flags and start them together. Stop the API servers during the restore so they do not write. After starting, check member health and that objects are present.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-of-a-backup\">Limits of a backup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An etcd snapshot restores Kubernetes objects. It does not contain the data in persistent volumes, container images, certificates stored outside the data directory unless you save them, or the state of external systems. It also restores old state: objects deleted since then reappear, and nodes may disagree with reality. Pair etcd backups with volume snapshots, a copy of the PKI directory, and manifests kept in a repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You restore a three-day-old snapshot. What might surprise you?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Objects created or changed in the last three days are gone, and objects deleted since then are back.\u003c/p>\u003cp>State rolls back to the snapshot time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backup-that-could-not-restore\">Incident: The backup that could not restore\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cluster lost two of three etcd members and the team tried to restore the latest nightly snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"etcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-latest.db -w table\nls -l /backup | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>etcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-latest.db -w table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: file is corrupt or truncated\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /backup | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 May 13 02:00 etcd-2024-05-13.db\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The copy job had been failing for a month and writing empty files. The team restored from an older valid file, then added a check that verifies each snapshot with the status command, alerts on size and age, and runs a quarterly restore drill in a test cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Never testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving snapshots on the same node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that etcd holds Secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming etcd backup covers volumes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"taking-a-snapshot","text":"Taking a snapshot"},{"id":"store-it-well","text":"Store it well"},{"id":"restoring","text":"Restoring"},{"id":"limits-of-a-backup","text":"Limits of a backup"},{"id":"incident-the-backup-that-could-not-restore","text":"Incident: The backup that could not restore"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Operating etcd clusters for Kubernetes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/"},{"t":"Administer a cluster","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/"}],"text":"If you lose etcd and have no backup, you lose the definition of your cluster. A backup you have never restored is only a hope. This lesson covers taking snapshots, storing them safely, restoring into a new cluster, and testing the whole procedure on a schedule. What you will learn Take an etcd snapshot Store and protect backups Restore from a snapshot Test restores and know what a backup does not cover Taking a snapshot Use etcdctl with the endpoints and client certificates of your cluster: ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-$( date +%F).db \\ --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\ --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\ --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\ --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key etcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2024-05-14.db -w table The status command shows the revision, key count and size, which proves the file is readable. Managed Kubernetes services hide etcd; use the provider&#39;s backup features or a tool that backs up objects and volumes. Store it well Copy snapshots off the node at once, to storage in another location, encrypted and with limited access, because etcd holds Secrets. Keep several generations and a retention policy. Schedule snapshots often enough for your recovery point: hourly is common. Alert if the newest snapshot is older than expected. Restoring Restoring creates a new data directory from the snapshot with etcdutl snapshot restore , then the etcd static pod or service is pointed at it. On a multi-member cluster, restore every member from the same snapshot with the new cluster flags and start them together. Stop the API servers during the restore so they do not write. After starting, check member health and that objects are present. Limits of a backup An etcd snapshot restores Kubernetes objects. It does not contain the data in persistent volumes, container images, certificates stored outside the data directory unless you save them, or the state of external systems. It also restores old state: objects deleted since then reappear, and nodes may disagree with reality. Pair etcd backups with volume snapshots, a copy of the PKI directory, and manifests kept in a repository. Predict first. You restore a three-day-old snapshot. What might surprise you? Your guess Answer: Objects created or changed in the last three days are gone, and objects deleted since then are back. State rolls back to the snapshot time. Incident: The backup that could not restore Incident A cluster lost two of three etcd members and the team tried to restore the latest nightly snapshot. $ etcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-latest.db -w table Error: file is corrupt or truncated $ ls -l /backup | tail -3 -rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 May 13 02:00 etcd-2024-05-13.db The copy job had been failing for a month and writing empty files. The team restored from an older valid file, then added a check that verifies each snapshot with the status command, alerts on size and age, and runs a quarterly restore drill in a test cluster. Common mistakes Never testing a restore Leaving snapshots on the same node Forgetting that etcd holds Secrets Assuming etcd backup covers volumes"},"k8s-ops/upgrading-clusters-safely":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes ships a new minor version about three times a year, and each version is supported for roughly a year. Staying on a supported version is a security and reliability task. Upgrades are routine when you plan them and painful when they are a surprise after two years.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Respect the supported upgrade path and version skew\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Find deprecated APIs before they break\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrade the control plane, then nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prepare a rollback and test in a lower environment\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-minor-version-at-a-time\">One minor version at a time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You upgrade from 1.N to 1.N+1, not across several minors at once. The control plane is upgraded first. Kubelets may be older than the API server by a limited number of minor versions (check the version skew policy for the current allowance) but never newer. Components such as kubectl, CNI plugins, ingress controllers and operators have their own compatibility tables: read them before you start.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deprecated-apis\">Deprecated APIs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each release removes some API versions. A manifest using a removed version fails to apply. Before upgrading, scan the cluster and your repositories for deprecated APIs with tools such as \u003ccode>kubectl get --raw /metrics | grep apiserver_requested_deprecated_apis\u003c/code> or dedicated scanners, update manifests and charts, and read the release notes for removals and behaviour changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"control-plane-then-nodes\">Control plane then nodes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With kubeadm: upgrade the first control plane node (\u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade plan\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code>), then the others with \u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade node\u003c/code>, then upgrade kubelet and kubectl packages and restart the kubelet. For worker nodes, upgrade one or a few at a time: \u003cstrong>cordon\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>drain\u003c/strong> the node, upgrade it, \u003cstrong>uncordon\u003c/strong>. Managed services automate much of this; you still choose the timing and check workload health. An alternative is the surge approach: add new nodes at the new version and drain the old ones.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rollback-and-rehearsal\">Rollback and rehearsal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Control plane upgrades are hard to undo, so the real rollback plan is a restored etcd snapshot and a documented procedure. Take a fresh snapshot first. Rehearse in a staging cluster that matches production, and run smoke tests after each step. Use PodDisruptionBudgets so draining does not take down a service, and schedule the window when traffic is low.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A manifest uses an API version removed in the next release. When do you find out the cheapest way?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Before the upgrade, by scanning the cluster and repositories for deprecated API use.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check first, not after.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-ingress-objects-stopped-working\">Incident: Ingress objects stopped working\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After an upgrade, a team&#39;s routes disappeared and the pipeline failed to apply them.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml\nkubectl api-versions | grep networking.k8s.io\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: unable to recognize &quot;ingress.yaml&quot;: no matches for kind &quot;Ingress&quot; in version &quot;extensions/v1beta1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl api-versions | grep networking.k8s.io\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The old API version had been removed. The team updated the manifests to the stable version, added a deprecation scan to the pipeline, and wrote the check into the upgrade checklist so the next release would show problems weeks earlier.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping minor versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring plugin compatibility\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading without a snapshot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining without disruption budgets\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"one-minor-version-at-a-time","text":"One minor version at a time"},{"id":"deprecated-apis","text":"Deprecated APIs"},{"id":"control-plane-then-nodes","text":"Control plane then nodes"},{"id":"rollback-and-rehearsal","text":"Rollback and rehearsal"},{"id":"incident-ingress-objects-stopped-working","text":"Incident: Ingress objects stopped working"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Upgrading kubeadm clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-upgrade/"},{"t":"Version skew policy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/releases/version-skew-policy/"}],"text":"Kubernetes ships a new minor version about three times a year, and each version is supported for roughly a year. Staying on a supported version is a security and reliability task. Upgrades are routine when you plan them and painful when they are a surprise after two years. What you will learn Respect the supported upgrade path and version skew Find deprecated APIs before they break Upgrade the control plane, then nodes Prepare a rollback and test in a lower environment One minor version at a time You upgrade from 1.N to 1.N+1, not across several minors at once. The control plane is upgraded first. Kubelets may be older than the API server by a limited number of minor versions (check the version skew policy for the current allowance) but never newer. Components such as kubectl, CNI plugins, ingress controllers and operators have their own compatibility tables: read them before you start. Deprecated APIs Each release removes some API versions. A manifest using a removed version fails to apply. Before upgrading, scan the cluster and your repositories for deprecated APIs with tools such as kubectl get --raw /metrics | grep apiserver_requested_deprecated_apis or dedicated scanners, update manifests and charts, and read the release notes for removals and behaviour changes. Control plane then nodes With kubeadm: upgrade the first control plane node ( kubeadm upgrade plan , then apply ), then the others with kubeadm upgrade node , then upgrade kubelet and kubectl packages and restart the kubelet. For worker nodes, upgrade one or a few at a time: cordon and drain the node, upgrade it, uncordon . Managed services automate much of this; you still choose the timing and check workload health. An alternative is the surge approach: add new nodes at the new version and drain the old ones. Rollback and rehearsal Control plane upgrades are hard to undo, so the real rollback plan is a restored etcd snapshot and a documented procedure. Take a fresh snapshot first. Rehearse in a staging cluster that matches production, and run smoke tests after each step. Use PodDisruptionBudgets so draining does not take down a service, and schedule the window when traffic is low. Predict first. A manifest uses an API version removed in the next release. When do you find out the cheapest way? Your guess Answer: Before the upgrade, by scanning the cluster and repositories for deprecated API use. Check first, not after. Incident: Ingress objects stopped working Incident After an upgrade, a team&#39;s routes disappeared and the pipeline failed to apply them. $ kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml error: unable to recognize \"ingress.yaml\": no matches for kind \"Ingress\" in version \"extensions/v1beta1\" $ kubectl api-versions | grep networking.k8s.io networking.k8s.io/v1 The old API version had been removed. The team updated the manifests to the stable version, added a deprecation scan to the pipeline, and wrote the check into the upgrade checklist so the next release would show problems weeks earlier. Common mistakes Skipping minor versions Ignoring plugin compatibility Upgrading without a snapshot Draining without disruption budgets"},"k8s-ops/rbac-and-service-accounts":{"html":"\u003cp>Role-based access control decides what each identity may do with the Kubernetes API. Pods use service accounts to call that API as well. Poor RBAC is a common cause of both outages (a controller cannot do its job) and breaches (everyone is cluster-admin). The model is simple once you see its four objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Write Roles, ClusterRoles and bindings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose namespace-wide or cluster-wide scope\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use service accounts for workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audit permissions with kubectl auth can-i\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"four-objects\">Four objects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>Role\u003c/strong> lists allowed verbs on resources inside one namespace. A \u003cstrong>ClusterRole\u003c/strong> does the same cluster-wide or for non-namespaced resources like nodes. A \u003cstrong>RoleBinding\u003c/strong> gives a Role (or ClusterRole) to subjects in one namespace. A \u003cstrong>ClusterRoleBinding\u003c/strong> gives a ClusterRole to subjects everywhere. RBAC only adds permissions; there is no deny.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  name: pod-reader\n  namespace: shop\nrules:\n  - apiGroups: [&quot;&quot;]\n    resources: [&quot;pods&quot;, &quot;pods/log&quot;]\n    verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;watch&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Role\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pod-reader\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroups:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods/log&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verbs:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;watch&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"subjects\">Subjects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Subjects are users, groups and service accounts. Kubernetes does not store users: they come from your authentication method (certificates, OpenID Connect through a company identity provider). Bind groups rather than individuals so access follows team membership.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-accounts\">Service accounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each pod runs as a \u003cstrong>service account\u003c/strong>, whose short-lived token is mounted into the pod so the application can call the API. The default service account in a namespace should have no extra rights, and pods that never call the API should not mount a token (\u003ccode>automountServiceAccountToken: false\u003c/code>). Give each application its own service account with only the verbs it needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"avoid-broad-rights\">Avoid broad rights\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Be careful with wildcards (\u003ccode>*\u003c/code>), the \u003ccode>cluster-admin\u003c/code> role, and powerful verbs such as \u003ccode>create\u003c/code> on pods, \u003ccode>escalate\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bind\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>impersonate\u003c/code>, and reading Secrets cluster-wide: each can be used to gain more access. Review ClusterRoleBindings regularly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"checking-access\">Checking access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ask the cluster: \u003ccode>kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as=jane\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>kubectl auth can-i --list -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:web\u003c/code>. These answer questions without trial and error.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pod needs to list pods in its own namespace only. What should you create?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A service account, a Role allowing get/list/watch on pods, and a RoleBinding in that namespace.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Smallest scope.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-forbidden-for-the-controller\">Incident: Forbidden for the controller\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A custom controller crashed after deployment with permission errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs deploy/cert-sync | tail -2\nkubectl auth can-i list secrets -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:ops:cert-sync\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/cert-sync | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error from server (Forbidden): secrets is forbidden: User &quot;system:serviceaccount:ops:cert-sync&quot; cannot list resource &quot;secrets&quot; in API group &quot;&quot; in the namespace &quot;shop&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i list secrets -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:ops:cert-sync\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">no\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The error named the exact subject, verb and resource. The team added a Role for that one verb in that one namespace and a binding, and did not use cluster-admin. They then checked the other namespaces with can-i --list.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Binding cluster-admin to fix errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the default service account for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting wildcards\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never reviewing bindings\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"four-objects","text":"Four objects"},{"id":"subjects","text":"Subjects"},{"id":"service-accounts","text":"Service accounts"},{"id":"avoid-broad-rights","text":"Avoid broad rights"},{"id":"checking-access","text":"Checking access"},{"id":"incident-forbidden-for-the-controller","text":"Incident: Forbidden for the controller"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Using RBAC authorization","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/"},{"t":"Service accounts","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/"}],"text":"Role-based access control decides what each identity may do with the Kubernetes API. Pods use service accounts to call that API as well. Poor RBAC is a common cause of both outages (a controller cannot do its job) and breaches (everyone is cluster-admin). The model is simple once you see its four objects. What you will learn Write Roles, ClusterRoles and bindings Choose namespace-wide or cluster-wide scope Use service accounts for workloads Audit permissions with kubectl auth can-i Four objects A Role lists allowed verbs on resources inside one namespace. A ClusterRole does the same cluster-wide or for non-namespaced resources like nodes. A RoleBinding gives a Role (or ClusterRole) to subjects in one namespace. A ClusterRoleBinding gives a ClusterRole to subjects everywhere. RBAC only adds permissions; there is no deny. apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata: name: pod-reader namespace: shop rules: - apiGroups: [ \"\" ] resources: [ \"pods\" , \"pods/log\" ] verbs: [ \"get\" , \"list\" , \"watch\" ] Subjects Subjects are users, groups and service accounts. Kubernetes does not store users: they come from your authentication method (certificates, OpenID Connect through a company identity provider). Bind groups rather than individuals so access follows team membership. Service accounts Each pod runs as a service account , whose short-lived token is mounted into the pod so the application can call the API. The default service account in a namespace should have no extra rights, and pods that never call the API should not mount a token ( automountServiceAccountToken: false ). Give each application its own service account with only the verbs it needs. Avoid broad rights Be careful with wildcards ( * ), the cluster-admin role, and powerful verbs such as create on pods, escalate , bind , impersonate , and reading Secrets cluster-wide: each can be used to gain more access. Review ClusterRoleBindings regularly. Checking access Ask the cluster: kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as=jane and kubectl auth can-i --list -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:web . These answer questions without trial and error. Predict first. A pod needs to list pods in its own namespace only. What should you create? Your guess Answer: A service account, a Role allowing get/list/watch on pods, and a RoleBinding in that namespace. Smallest scope. Incident: Forbidden for the controller Incident A custom controller crashed after deployment with permission errors. $ kubectl logs deploy/cert-sync | tail -2 Error from server (Forbidden): secrets is forbidden: User \"system:serviceaccount:ops:cert-sync\" cannot list resource \"secrets\" in API group \"\" in the namespace \"shop\" $ kubectl auth can-i list secrets -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:ops:cert-sync no The error named the exact subject, verb and resource. The team added a Role for that one verb in that one namespace and a binding, and did not use cluster-admin. They then checked the other namespaces with can-i --list. Common mistakes Binding cluster-admin to fix errors Using the default service account for everything Granting wildcards Never reviewing bindings"},"k8s-ops/nodes-draining-taints-and-capacity":{"html":"\u003cp>Nodes need patching, replacing and resizing. Doing this without dropping traffic depends on understanding how Kubernetes evicts pods, respects disruption budgets and places workloads. It also helps to keep special nodes (GPUs, large memory, spot capacity) for the workloads that need them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cordon, drain and uncordon\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use PodDisruptionBudgets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Control placement with taints, tolerations and affinity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read node capacity and allocatable resources\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cordon-and-drain\">Cordon and drain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl cordon node-1\u003c/code> marks the node unschedulable but leaves pods running. \u003ccode>kubectl drain node-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\u003c/code> evicts pods so they are recreated elsewhere, respecting their disruption budgets. After maintenance, \u003ccode>kubectl uncordon node-1\u003c/code> makes it schedulable again. Pods that are not managed by a controller are not recreated, so drain warns about them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"poddisruptionbudgets\">PodDisruptionBudgets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>PodDisruptionBudget\u003c/strong> says how many replicas of an application must remain available (\u003ccode>minAvailable: 2\u003c/code>) or may be unavailable (\u003ccode>maxUnavailable: 1\u003c/code>) during voluntary disruptions like drains. A drain waits instead of violating the budget. A budget that requires all replicas makes drains hang forever, and with a single replica a strict budget blocks maintenance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"taints-and-tolerations\">Taints and tolerations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>taint\u003c/strong> on a node repels pods unless they have a matching \u003cstrong>toleration\u003c/strong>. For example, \u003ccode>kubectl taint nodes gpu-1 gpu=true:NoSchedule\u003c/code> keeps ordinary pods away from GPU nodes; GPU workloads tolerate the taint. Effects are NoSchedule, PreferNoSchedule and NoExecute (which also evicts running pods). Kubernetes itself adds taints such as \u003ccode>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"affinity-and-spread\">Affinity and spread\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Node affinity attracts pods to labelled nodes. Pod anti-affinity and topology spread constraints spread replicas across nodes and zones so one failure does not remove them all. Taints keep others out; affinity pulls pods in; use both for dedicated pools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity\">Capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl describe node\u003c/code> shows \u003cstrong>capacity\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>allocatable\u003c/strong> (what pods can use after system reservations) and what is already requested. Scheduling uses requests, not usage. Leave headroom for draining a node: if the cluster cannot absorb the pods of one node, maintenance will leave pods Pending.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A drain hangs on a Deployment with 2 replicas and a PodDisruptionBudget of minAvailable 2. Why?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The budget requires both replicas, so no pod may be evicted. Lower the budget or add a replica.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Budgets can block drains.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-drain-that-never-finished\">Incident: The drain that never finished\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Node maintenance stalled for an hour with the message &quot;Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod&#39;s disruption budget&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl drain node-3 --ignore-daemonsets\nkubectl get pdb -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl drain node-3 --ignore-daemonsets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error when evicting pods/&quot;api-7f6c&quot; -n shop: Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod's disruption budget.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pdb -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME  MIN AVAILABLE  ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api   3              0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The deployment had 3 replicas and required 3. The team scaled it to 4, changed the budget to minAvailable 2, and the drain continued. They added a review check for budgets that allow zero disruptions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>PDBs that allow zero disruptions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining without enough spare capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting daemonsets and local data flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using taints without tolerations\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cordon-and-drain","text":"Cordon and drain"},{"id":"poddisruptionbudgets","text":"PodDisruptionBudgets"},{"id":"taints-and-tolerations","text":"Taints and tolerations"},{"id":"affinity-and-spread","text":"Affinity and spread"},{"id":"capacity","text":"Capacity"},{"id":"incident-the-drain-that-never-finished","text":"Incident: The drain that never finished"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Safely drain a node","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/"},{"t":"Taints and tolerations","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/"}],"text":"Nodes need patching, replacing and resizing. Doing this without dropping traffic depends on understanding how Kubernetes evicts pods, respects disruption budgets and places workloads. It also helps to keep special nodes (GPUs, large memory, spot capacity) for the workloads that need them. What you will learn Cordon, drain and uncordon Use PodDisruptionBudgets Control placement with taints, tolerations and affinity Read node capacity and allocatable resources Cordon and drain kubectl cordon node-1 marks the node unschedulable but leaves pods running. kubectl drain node-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data evicts pods so they are recreated elsewhere, respecting their disruption budgets. After maintenance, kubectl uncordon node-1 makes it schedulable again. Pods that are not managed by a controller are not recreated, so drain warns about them. PodDisruptionBudgets A PodDisruptionBudget says how many replicas of an application must remain available ( minAvailable: 2 ) or may be unavailable ( maxUnavailable: 1 ) during voluntary disruptions like drains. A drain waits instead of violating the budget. A budget that requires all replicas makes drains hang forever, and with a single replica a strict budget blocks maintenance. Taints and tolerations A taint on a node repels pods unless they have a matching toleration . For example, kubectl taint nodes gpu-1 gpu=true:NoSchedule keeps ordinary pods away from GPU nodes; GPU workloads tolerate the taint. Effects are NoSchedule, PreferNoSchedule and NoExecute (which also evicts running pods). Kubernetes itself adds taints such as node.kubernetes.io/not-ready . Affinity and spread Node affinity attracts pods to labelled nodes. Pod anti-affinity and topology spread constraints spread replicas across nodes and zones so one failure does not remove them all. Taints keep others out; affinity pulls pods in; use both for dedicated pools. Capacity kubectl describe node shows capacity , allocatable (what pods can use after system reservations) and what is already requested. Scheduling uses requests, not usage. Leave headroom for draining a node: if the cluster cannot absorb the pods of one node, maintenance will leave pods Pending. Predict first. A drain hangs on a Deployment with 2 replicas and a PodDisruptionBudget of minAvailable 2. Why? Your guess Answer: The budget requires both replicas, so no pod may be evicted. Lower the budget or add a replica. Budgets can block drains. Incident: The drain that never finished Incident Node maintenance stalled for an hour with the message \"Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod&#39;s disruption budget\". $ kubectl drain node-3 --ignore-daemonsets error when evicting pods/\"api-7f6c\" -n shop: Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod's disruption budget. $ kubectl get pdb -n shop NAME MIN AVAILABLE ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS api 3 0 The deployment had 3 replicas and required 3. The team scaled it to 4, changed the budget to minAvailable 2, and the drain continued. They added a review check for budgets that allow zero disruptions. Common mistakes PDBs that allow zero disruptions Draining without enough spare capacity Forgetting daemonsets and local data flags Using taints without tolerations"},"k8s-ops/resource-quotas-limits-and-autoscaling":{"html":"\u003cp>A shared cluster needs rules about how much each team and workload can use. Without them, one runaway deployment can starve everything else. With sensible requests, limits, quotas and autoscaling, the cluster stays predictable and cost under control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Set requests and limits and know their effects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply ResourceQuotas and LimitRanges per namespace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand node autoscaling\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requests-and-limits\">Requests and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>request\u003c/strong> is what the scheduler reserves; a \u003cstrong>limit\u003c/strong> is the ceiling. Exceeding the memory limit gets a container OOM-killed. Exceeding the CPU limit throttles it. The ratio of requests to limits sets the quality of service class: Guaranteed (equal), Burstable, or BestEffort (none), which is evicted first under pressure. Many teams set memory request equal to limit, and set CPU requests without a tight CPU limit to avoid throttling.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quotas-and-defaults\">Quotas and defaults\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>ResourceQuota\u003c/strong> caps the total CPU, memory, storage or object counts in a namespace. A \u003cstrong>LimitRange\u003c/strong> sets default requests and limits and minimums or maximums per container, so pods without values still get sensible ones. With a quota on CPU or memory, pods must declare those resources or be rejected.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ResourceQuota\nmetadata:\n  name: team-a\n  namespace: team-a\nspec:\n  hard:\n    requests.cpu: &quot;20&quot;\n    requests.memory: 64Gi\n    pods: &quot;100&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ResourceQuota\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team-a\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team-a\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hard:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests.cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;20&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests.memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">64Gi\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pods:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;100&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"horizontal-pod-autoscaler\">Horizontal Pod Autoscaler\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The HPA changes the replica count based on metrics such as CPU use compared with the request, or custom metrics. It needs the metrics server and CPU requests set. Set sensible minimum and maximum replicas, and remember it reacts over minutes: for sudden spikes keep enough baseline. Vertical autoscaling resizes requests and is useful for right-sizing, but changes may restart pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"node-autoscaling\">Node autoscaling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The cluster autoscaler or Karpenter adds nodes when pods are Pending for lack of capacity and removes under-used ones. It relies on accurate requests: if requests are inflated you pay for idle nodes, and if they are too low pods are packed too tightly and suffer. Watch for pods that cannot be moved (local storage, strict budgets) which block scale-down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pod is killed with reason OOMKilled. What changed or is wrong?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It exceeded its memory limit; raise the limit based on observed usage or fix a memory leak.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Memory limits are hard.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-namespace-full\">Incident: Namespace full\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment could not create pods and showed &quot;exceeded quota&quot; events.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe quota team-a -n team-a\nkubectl get events -n team-a | tail -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe quota team-a -n team-a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Resource          Used  Hard\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">requests.memory   63Gi  64Gi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n team-a | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FailedCreate  exceeded quota: team-a, requested: requests.memory=4Gi, used: 63Gi, limited: 64Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had grown beyond its quota. They checked actual use, found three over-sized deployments, reduced their requests, and agreed a higher quota through a review rather than deleting the limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No requests set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CPU limits that cause throttling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quotas without defaults\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>HPA without requests\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"requests-and-limits","text":"Requests and limits"},{"id":"quotas-and-defaults","text":"Quotas and defaults"},{"id":"horizontal-pod-autoscaler","text":"Horizontal Pod Autoscaler"},{"id":"node-autoscaling","text":"Node autoscaling"},{"id":"incident-namespace-full","text":"Incident: Namespace full"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Resource quotas","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas/"},{"t":"Horizontal Pod Autoscaling","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/"}],"text":"A shared cluster needs rules about how much each team and workload can use. Without them, one runaway deployment can starve everything else. With sensible requests, limits, quotas and autoscaling, the cluster stays predictable and cost under control. What you will learn Set requests and limits and know their effects Apply ResourceQuotas and LimitRanges per namespace Use the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler Understand node autoscaling Requests and limits A request is what the scheduler reserves; a limit is the ceiling. Exceeding the memory limit gets a container OOM-killed. Exceeding the CPU limit throttles it. The ratio of requests to limits sets the quality of service class: Guaranteed (equal), Burstable, or BestEffort (none), which is evicted first under pressure. Many teams set memory request equal to limit, and set CPU requests without a tight CPU limit to avoid throttling. Quotas and defaults A ResourceQuota caps the total CPU, memory, storage or object counts in a namespace. A LimitRange sets default requests and limits and minimums or maximums per container, so pods without values still get sensible ones. With a quota on CPU or memory, pods must declare those resources or be rejected. apiVersion: v1 kind: ResourceQuota metadata: name: team-a namespace: team-a spec: hard: requests.cpu: \"20\" requests.memory: 64Gi pods: \"100\" Horizontal Pod Autoscaler The HPA changes the replica count based on metrics such as CPU use compared with the request, or custom metrics. It needs the metrics server and CPU requests set. Set sensible minimum and maximum replicas, and remember it reacts over minutes: for sudden spikes keep enough baseline. Vertical autoscaling resizes requests and is useful for right-sizing, but changes may restart pods. Node autoscaling The cluster autoscaler or Karpenter adds nodes when pods are Pending for lack of capacity and removes under-used ones. It relies on accurate requests: if requests are inflated you pay for idle nodes, and if they are too low pods are packed too tightly and suffer. Watch for pods that cannot be moved (local storage, strict budgets) which block scale-down. Predict first. A pod is killed with reason OOMKilled. What changed or is wrong? Your guess Answer: It exceeded its memory limit; raise the limit based on observed usage or fix a memory leak. Memory limits are hard. Incident: Namespace full Incident A deployment could not create pods and showed \"exceeded quota\" events. $ kubectl describe quota team-a -n team-a Resource Used Hard requests.memory 63Gi 64Gi $ kubectl get events -n team-a | tail -1 FailedCreate exceeded quota: team-a, requested: requests.memory=4Gi, used: 63Gi, limited: 64Gi The team had grown beyond its quota. They checked actual use, found three over-sized deployments, reduced their requests, and agreed a higher quota through a review rather than deleting the limit. Common mistakes No requests set CPU limits that cause throttling Quotas without defaults HPA without requests"},"k8s-ops/network-policies-and-pod-security":{"html":"\u003cp>By default every pod can talk to every other pod, and a container can ask for powers it should not have. Two features close those gaps: NetworkPolicies limit traffic, and Pod Security Admission limits what pods can request. Together they contain damage when something is compromised.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Write a default-deny NetworkPolicy and add allowances\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know that a policy needs a network plugin that enforces it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply Pod Security Standards by namespace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Harden containers with a security context\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"default-deny-then-allow\">Default deny, then allow\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NetworkPolicy selects pods and lists allowed ingress and egress. Once any policy selects a pod for a direction, only listed traffic is allowed in that direction.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [&quot;Ingress&quot;, &quot;Egress&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default-deny\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Ingress&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Egress&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Then add narrow policies, for instance letting the web pods reach the API pods on one port, and allowing DNS egress. Remember DNS or everything breaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"enforcement\">Enforcement\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Policies are enforced by the network plugin (such as Calico or Cilium). If your plugin does not support them, the objects are accepted but do nothing. Test with a pod that should be blocked and confirm it is.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pod-security-standards\">Pod Security Standards\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Three profiles: \u003cstrong>privileged\u003c/strong> (no limits), \u003cstrong>baseline\u003c/strong> (blocks known escalations such as privileged containers and host namespaces) and \u003cstrong>restricted\u003c/strong> (current hardening practice: non-root, no privilege escalation, dropped capabilities, seccomp). Pod Security Admission applies them per namespace with labels in modes enforce, audit and warn.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security-context\">Security context\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set it on pods and containers: \u003ccode>runAsNonRoot: true\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>capabilities: { drop: [&quot;ALL&quot;] }\u003c/code> and a seccomp profile. Avoid \u003ccode>hostNetwork\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>hostPID\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>hostPath\u003c/code> and privileged mode. Add policy engines such as Kyverno or OPA Gatekeeper for rules beyond the built-in profiles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You apply a default-deny egress policy and every pod stops resolving names. What did you forget?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> An allowance for DNS egress to the cluster DNS pods.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Allow DNS.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-lateral-movement\">Incident: Lateral movement\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A compromised front-end pod scanned the cluster and reached the database pods directly.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec web-5d -- nc -zv db.shop.svc 5432\nkubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-5d -- nc -zv db.shop.svc 5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db.shop.svc (10.96.14.7:5432) open\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">No resources found in shop namespace.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>There were no policies, so every pod could reach the database. The team added default deny, allowed only the API pods to reach the database on its port, enabled the restricted profile for new namespaces, and tested that web pods are now blocked.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming policies work without a supporting plugin\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting DNS in egress rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running containers as root\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Privileged pods for convenience\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"default-deny-then-allow","text":"Default deny, then allow"},{"id":"enforcement","text":"Enforcement"},{"id":"pod-security-standards","text":"Pod Security Standards"},{"id":"security-context","text":"Security context"},{"id":"incident-lateral-movement","text":"Incident: Lateral movement"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Network policies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"},{"t":"Pod Security Standards","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/"}],"text":"By default every pod can talk to every other pod, and a container can ask for powers it should not have. Two features close those gaps: NetworkPolicies limit traffic, and Pod Security Admission limits what pods can request. Together they contain damage when something is compromised. What you will learn Write a default-deny NetworkPolicy and add allowances Know that a policy needs a network plugin that enforces it Apply Pod Security Standards by namespace Harden containers with a security context Default deny, then allow A NetworkPolicy selects pods and lists allowed ingress and egress. Once any policy selects a pod for a direction, only listed traffic is allowed in that direction. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [ \"Ingress\" , \"Egress\" ] Then add narrow policies, for instance letting the web pods reach the API pods on one port, and allowing DNS egress. Remember DNS or everything breaks. Enforcement Policies are enforced by the network plugin (such as Calico or Cilium). If your plugin does not support them, the objects are accepted but do nothing. Test with a pod that should be blocked and confirm it is. Pod Security Standards Three profiles: privileged (no limits), baseline (blocks known escalations such as privileged containers and host namespaces) and restricted (current hardening practice: non-root, no privilege escalation, dropped capabilities, seccomp). Pod Security Admission applies them per namespace with labels in modes enforce, audit and warn. kubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted Security context Set it on pods and containers: runAsNonRoot: true , allowPrivilegeEscalation: false , readOnlyRootFilesystem: true , capabilities: { drop: [\"ALL\"] } and a seccomp profile. Avoid hostNetwork , hostPID , hostPath and privileged mode. Add policy engines such as Kyverno or OPA Gatekeeper for rules beyond the built-in profiles. Predict first. You apply a default-deny egress policy and every pod stops resolving names. What did you forget? Your guess Answer: An allowance for DNS egress to the cluster DNS pods. Allow DNS. Incident: Lateral movement Incident A compromised front-end pod scanned the cluster and reached the database pods directly. $ kubectl exec web-5d -- nc -zv db.shop.svc 5432 db.shop.svc (10.96.14.7:5432) open $ kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop No resources found in shop namespace. There were no policies, so every pod could reach the database. The team added default deny, allowed only the API pods to reach the database on its port, enabled the restricted profile for new namespaces, and tested that web pods are now blocked. Common mistakes Assuming policies work without a supporting plugin Forgetting DNS in egress rules Running containers as root Privileged pods for convenience"},"k8s-ops/debugging-cluster-and-node-problems":{"html":"\u003cp>Cluster problems feel dramatic, but they respond to an ordered method: confirm the symptom, find the layer (workload, node, control plane, network), gather evidence, change one thing, and verify. This lesson gives you the checks and commands for the failures you will meet most.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Triage from workload to node to control plane\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnose a NotReady node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnose Pending pods and evictions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check the control plane and API server health\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"order-of-checks\">Order of checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start at the symptom. A pod problem: \u003ccode>kubectl describe pod\u003c/code> (events), \u003ccode>kubectl logs --previous\u003c/code>. If the pod is fine, look at the node: \u003ccode>kubectl get nodes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>kubectl describe node\u003c/code>. If several nodes or the API misbehave, look at the control plane. Resist changing things before you know which layer is at fault.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"notready-nodes\">NotReady nodes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl describe node\u003c/code> shows conditions: Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure, NetworkUnavailable. On the node, check \u003ccode>systemctl status kubelet\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>journalctl -u kubelet -n 100\u003c/code>, the container runtime (\u003ccode>crictl ps\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>systemctl status containerd\u003c/code>), disk space and inodes (\u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>df -i\u003c/code>), memory, and the network plugin pods. Common causes: full disk, a stopped kubelet or runtime, expired certificates, or lost network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pending-and-evicted-pods\">Pending and evicted pods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Pending pod shows the reason in events: insufficient CPU or memory, node selector or affinity mismatch, untolerated taints, unbound volume claims. Evicted pods usually indicate node pressure (often ephemeral storage or memory); look at which pods use the most and at requests. CrashLoopBackOff and ImagePullBackOff are workload-level problems, not node-level.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"control-plane-health\">Control plane health\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check \u003ccode>kubectl get --raw=/readyz?verbose\u003c/code>, API server logs, etcd health and disk latency, and certificate expiry (\u003ccode>kubeadm certs check-expiration\u003c/code>). A slow API often comes from etcd latency, an overloaded API server due to a misbehaving client making huge list calls, or admission webhooks that time out and block writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"close-the-loop\">Close the loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record the timeline, the cause, the fix and a prevention (an alert on disk usage or certificate expiry, a quota, a runbook). Practise these in a test cluster so the first time is not production.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A node is NotReady and kubelet logs say &quot;no space left on device&quot;. What do you check next?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Disk usage and inodes on the node, especially image storage and logs, then free space or resize.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Common cause.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-certificates-expired\">Incident: Certificates expired\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>All kubectl commands failed with a certificate error one morning.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes\nsudo kubeadm certs check-expiration | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm certs check-expiration | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CERTIFICATE  EXPIRES  RESIDUAL TIME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">admin.conf   Mar 01, 2025 02:11 UTC  &lt;invalid&gt;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cluster certificates, issued a year earlier, had expired. The team renewed them with kubeadm, restarted the control plane components, then added a monitor on expiry dates and an annual upgrade schedule that renews them automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Restarting things before looking at events\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring node conditions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No monitoring of certificate expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging the wrong layer\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"order-of-checks","text":"Order of checks"},{"id":"notready-nodes","text":"NotReady nodes"},{"id":"pending-and-evicted-pods","text":"Pending and evicted pods"},{"id":"control-plane-health","text":"Control plane health"},{"id":"close-the-loop","text":"Close the loop"},{"id":"incident-certificates-expired","text":"Incident: Certificates expired"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Troubleshooting clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/"},{"t":"Debug running pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-running-pod/"}],"text":"Cluster problems feel dramatic, but they respond to an ordered method: confirm the symptom, find the layer (workload, node, control plane, network), gather evidence, change one thing, and verify. This lesson gives you the checks and commands for the failures you will meet most. What you will learn Triage from workload to node to control plane Diagnose a NotReady node Diagnose Pending pods and evictions Check the control plane and API server health Order of checks Start at the symptom. A pod problem: kubectl describe pod (events), kubectl logs --previous . If the pod is fine, look at the node: kubectl get nodes , kubectl describe node . If several nodes or the API misbehave, look at the control plane. Resist changing things before you know which layer is at fault. NotReady nodes kubectl describe node shows conditions: Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure, NetworkUnavailable. On the node, check systemctl status kubelet , journalctl -u kubelet -n 100 , the container runtime ( crictl ps , systemctl status containerd ), disk space and inodes ( df -h , df -i ), memory, and the network plugin pods. Common causes: full disk, a stopped kubelet or runtime, expired certificates, or lost network. Pending and evicted pods A Pending pod shows the reason in events: insufficient CPU or memory, node selector or affinity mismatch, untolerated taints, unbound volume claims. Evicted pods usually indicate node pressure (often ephemeral storage or memory); look at which pods use the most and at requests. CrashLoopBackOff and ImagePullBackOff are workload-level problems, not node-level. Control plane health Check kubectl get --raw=/readyz?verbose , API server logs, etcd health and disk latency, and certificate expiry ( kubeadm certs check-expiration ). A slow API often comes from etcd latency, an overloaded API server due to a misbehaving client making huge list calls, or admission webhooks that time out and block writes. Close the loop Record the timeline, the cause, the fix and a prevention (an alert on disk usage or certificate expiry, a quota, a runbook). Practise these in a test cluster so the first time is not production. Predict first. A node is NotReady and kubelet logs say \"no space left on device\". What do you check next? Your guess Answer: Disk usage and inodes on the node, especially image storage and logs, then free space or resize. Common cause. Incident: Certificates expired Incident All kubectl commands failed with a certificate error one morning. $ kubectl get nodes Unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid $ sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration | head -4 CERTIFICATE EXPIRES RESIDUAL TIME admin.conf Mar 01, 2025 02:11 UTC \u003cinvalid> The cluster certificates, issued a year earlier, had expired. The team renewed them with kubeadm, restarted the control plane components, then added a monitor on expiry dates and an annual upgrade schedule that renews them automatically. Common mistakes Restarting things before looking at events Ignoring node conditions No monitoring of certificate expiry Debugging the wrong layer"}});